<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <application>
        <!--
            Debug-only: stop Firebase Analytics collection entirely.

            CI instrumentation was polluting the production GA4 property
            (nubecita-2a4c1). The nightly sweep runs
            `:app:connectedProductionDebugAndroidTest` — the PRODUCTION flavor,
            which binds real FirebaseAnalytics — and `applicationIdSuffix` is
            deliberately unset (see the note in app/build.gradle.kts), so those
            emulator runs report as net.kikin.nubecita and land in the same
            property as real installs.

            Measured 2026-09-13: device model `sdk_gphone64_x86_64` was the #1
            device by active users (21 users / 21 sessions in a 7-day window, a
            flat 3/day). Every CI day had activeUsers == newUsers, because a
            wiped AVD gets a fresh app-instance id and is therefore counted as a
            brand-new user on every run — inflating first_open / newUsers /
            activeUsers by roughly 30%, and with it the top of the activation
            funnel. Returning users (activeUsers − newUsers) were unaffected.

            Why the manifest flag and not setAnalyticsCollectionEnabled(false)
            from NubecitaApplication.onCreate: the contaminating events are the
            SDK's own automatic ones — first_open, session_start, app_remove,
            screen_view, user_engagement (the emulators emitted ZERO custom
            events). Those fire as part of Firebase initialization, so a runtime
            disable in onCreate races them and would let first_open through.
            The manifest value is read before the SDK starts collecting.

            For the same reason a Hilt @TestInstallIn swapping AnalyticsModule
            for NoOpAnalyticsClient does NOT fix this — that only silences our
            own AnalyticsClient calls, which were never the problem.

            `firebase_analytics_collection_enabled` (not the `..._deactivated`
            kill switch) on purpose: this is a soft default, so a developer
            verifying analytics instrumentation on a debug build can still flip
            it back on at runtime with
            FirebaseAnalytics.setAnalyticsCollectionEnabled(true).

            Scope is the `debug` build type, so it covers both benchDebug and
            productionDebug. Release builds are untouched. Crashlytics is
            deliberately left enabled — it is low-volume from CI and still
            useful for catching instrumentation crashes.
        -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="firebase_analytics_collection_enabled"
            android:value="false" />
    </application>

</manifest>
